  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nActive weather to continue this evening into the overnight with an\ninitial severe storm threat, then continuing with the potential for \nheavy rain and flooding. A wavy surface frontal boundary is bisecting\nILN/s area. Forcing will increase late in the afternoon into this \nevening as a short wave induces a wave of low pressure along the \nboundary. A modest 20-25 kt low level jet will accompany this wave. \n\nFor the initial severe threat, low-level vorticity along the frontal\nboundary is will combine with 25-30 kts effective shear and\n1500-2000J of mixed-layer CAPE to bring the chance of a few \nsupercells. Threat with these storms initially will be damaging wind\ngusts and large hail. \n\nPWAT values remain anomalously high around 2 inches and the warm \ncloud layer will be deep. This will lead to the potential for heavy \nrain and flooding along and north of the front. HREF is showing the \npotential for 1 to 3 inches stripes of rainfall overnight with LPMM \nindicating that local totals in excess of 4 inches will be possible. \nThe exact placement of the heavy rain axis is somewhat uncertain with\nthe most favored location along the southern periphery of the \nI-corridor. Have issued a flood watch for potential flash flooding \novernight and added a buffer of counties north and south due to the \nuncertainty of exact placement. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nCooler airmass will settle across the region. A deep mid level trough\ndigging southeast from the Upper MS Valley will close off as it \nmoves across the area midweek. At the surface, an inverted trough \nwill extend along the western slopes of the Appalachians. This \ncombination will result in showery conditions, particularly across \neastern counties. For the period Tuesday thru Friday - high\ntemperatures look to range from the mid/upper 60s north to the lower \n70s south. These readings will be close to 10 degrees below normal.",
